WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.635
-Our next guest stars --
is a talented actress who stars

00:00:02.635 --> 00:00:04.303
in "What We Do in the Shadows,"

00:00:04.303 --> 00:00:06.172
which airs Thursdays
at 10:00 p.m. on FX.

00:00:06.172 --> 00:00:08.174
Let's take a look.
-How is this my fault?

00:00:08.174 --> 00:00:10.610
-Because I said to you
that you're the one

00:00:10.610 --> 00:00:14.480
to be responsible
for feeding the sire!

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:17.917
-What have I done?!
[ Whimpers ]

00:00:17.917 --> 00:00:20.687
-I was meaning to. I even
wrote it on a sticky note --

00:00:20.687 --> 00:00:22.822
"Ask Nandor feed sire."

00:00:22.822 --> 00:00:25.524
But I never actually got around
to giving him the message.

00:00:25.524 --> 00:00:27.093
-[ Whimpering ]

00:00:27.093 --> 00:00:29.862
-So, you know,
I think it's very difficult

00:00:29.862 --> 00:00:32.798
to say who's at fault, really.

00:00:32.798 --> 00:00:35.101
-Please welcome to the show
Natasia Demetriou.

00:00:35.101 --> 00:00:39.772
How are you, Natasia?
-I'm good. Hello.

00:00:39.772 --> 00:00:41.307
We're finally --
We're finally Zooming.

00:00:41.307 --> 00:00:43.509
-This is so exciting
to have you here.

00:00:43.509 --> 00:00:45.511
I'm such a huge fan.
And thank you very much.

00:00:45.511 --> 00:00:47.013
You've taken Zoom
very seriously.

00:00:47.013 --> 00:00:49.014
You look absolutely stunning.

00:00:49.014 --> 00:00:51.684
-Yes, thank you. I didn't
make -- This is just --

00:00:51.684 --> 00:00:53.953
I didn't make an effort.
I was just wearing this.

00:00:53.953 --> 00:00:55.387
-Oh, really?
-Yeah.

00:00:55.387 --> 00:00:57.423
-That's just your "hanging
around the house" clothes?

00:00:57.423 --> 00:00:59.659
-Yeah. I just got an e-mail
saying you wanted to chat,

00:00:59.659 --> 00:01:01.394
so I was just like, "Okay."

00:01:01.394 --> 00:01:03.129
[ Laughter ]

00:01:03.129 --> 00:01:05.731
-This is a fantastic show,
for those who don't watch it,

00:01:05.731 --> 00:01:07.066
about vampires.

00:01:07.066 --> 00:01:10.102
And it has a giant fan base
of comedy fans.

00:01:10.102 --> 00:01:14.907
But I'm wondering if vampire
fans also enjoy the show.

00:01:14.907 --> 00:01:17.143
-Oh, yeah. Yeah. Big-time.

00:01:17.143 --> 00:01:19.278
Vampires, themselves,
aren't a huge fan,

00:01:19.278 --> 00:01:21.013
'cause we're kind of
exposing them.

00:01:21.013 --> 00:01:22.915
-Right.
-But -- And we're quite --

00:01:22.915 --> 00:01:24.751
It's quite a secret community.

00:01:24.751 --> 00:01:27.320
But, yeah, vampire fans love it.

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:31.024
And when I --
When I had Instagram,

00:01:31.024 --> 00:01:35.294
I'd get a lot of very delicious
DM's asking me,

00:01:35.294 --> 00:01:37.130
you know,
if I wanted to swap blood

00:01:37.130 --> 00:01:40.233
or send articles of clothing

00:01:40.233 --> 00:01:42.835
that may be covered in blood.

00:01:42.835 --> 00:01:46.305
-Is that maybe why it's
a former Instagram account,

00:01:46.305 --> 00:01:48.441
as opposed to an active?
-No.

00:01:48.441 --> 00:01:50.877
That was the only reason
why I kept it.

00:01:50.877 --> 00:01:53.813
And -- So I miss that.
I miss it.

00:01:53.813 --> 00:01:57.683
-So this is -- It must be --
You know, there was --

00:01:57.683 --> 00:01:59.585
I feel like
we're at the end of this,

00:01:59.585 --> 00:02:00.920
like, huge vampire boom.

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:02.589
It was, like, very serious
for a while.

00:02:02.589 --> 00:02:05.391
And this is the really nice,
like, sort of

00:02:05.391 --> 00:02:06.893
taking the piss out of vampires.

00:02:06.893 --> 00:02:09.362
Were you -- Did you read
any of the vampire books?

00:02:09.362 --> 00:02:11.663
Did you see any of the movies?

00:02:11.663 --> 00:02:13.899
-I was -- I mean, I --

00:02:13.899 --> 00:02:16.869
"Twilight" was my jam.

00:02:16.869 --> 00:02:20.239
I was -- I would --
I inhaled those books.

00:02:20.239 --> 00:02:22.242
And by that,
I didn't even read them.

00:02:22.242 --> 00:02:24.310
I just, like, was with them.

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:27.913
And then they just
became part of me.

00:02:27.913 --> 00:02:30.382
-Did you -- Are they good?

00:02:30.382 --> 00:02:34.053
Do you feel like I would enjoy
the "Twilight" books?

00:02:34.053 --> 00:02:36.389
-Do you love really good things?
-Yeah.

00:02:36.389 --> 00:02:39.893
-Then I think you'd
absolutely love them.

00:02:39.893 --> 00:02:42.428
'Cause they're just like --

00:02:42.428 --> 00:02:45.464
Because I know you're sort of
a brooding teenage girl.

00:02:45.464 --> 00:02:49.902
I think that you -- For me,
it was just like that state of,

00:02:49.902 --> 00:02:53.706
like, just being so horny
and so wanting a boyfriend

00:02:53.706 --> 00:02:58.277
and so wanting to -- I didn't --
And then you could just, like --

00:02:58.277 --> 00:03:00.112
I would just read them
and then just sit.

00:03:00.112 --> 00:03:02.849
But, like, the reality is
it was just me being a teenager.

00:03:02.849 --> 00:03:04.650
Like, sat staring at a wall,

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:07.287
thinking that the whole world
revolved around me.

00:03:07.287 --> 00:03:10.923
But that's what those books,
like, evoked. They're heaven.

00:03:10.923 --> 00:03:13.492
-Well, it seems
to tick all my boxes,

00:03:13.492 --> 00:03:16.429
so I will be running out
to the nearest open book store

00:03:16.429 --> 00:03:17.864
I can find.

00:03:17.864 --> 00:03:20.466
Is it fun for you,
playing an immortal character,

00:03:20.466 --> 00:03:25.238
to work on things
like your character's backstory?

00:03:25.238 --> 00:03:27.773
And have you brought
any of your personal life

00:03:27.773 --> 00:03:30.309
into the character?

00:03:30.309 --> 00:03:32.244
-It is so fun.

00:03:32.244 --> 00:03:34.680
It's the most fun because
you don't have to worry about,

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:37.583
like, storyline or narrative
or anything, 'cause you're like,

00:03:37.583 --> 00:03:41.420
"Eh, she's been alive
for 700 years, so sure."

00:03:41.420 --> 00:03:44.223
But, yeah, I --
Yeah, I've been --

00:03:44.223 --> 00:03:47.493
When I first got the part,
Jemaine was very --

00:03:47.493 --> 00:03:49.328
And Taika were very, like,

00:03:49.328 --> 00:03:52.699
"You should channel your
Cypriot, you know, heritage."

00:03:52.699 --> 00:03:54.700
Like, my dad's from Cyprus.

00:03:54.700 --> 00:03:56.435
And, yeah,
I channel that a lot

00:03:56.435 --> 00:04:00.005
'cause basically Cypriot people
are essentially just vampires.

00:04:00.005 --> 00:04:03.342
'Cause they are --
They just care about, like, sex

00:04:03.342 --> 00:04:09.182
and, like, gold coins,
fire, and, like, death.

00:04:09.182 --> 00:04:11.417
If that is -- And that's --

00:04:11.417 --> 00:04:13.486
You know, like,
and it's such a rich --

00:04:13.486 --> 00:04:15.421
It's like the sort of
culture that just, like --

00:04:15.421 --> 00:04:17.790
My dad's lived in the U.K.
for most of his life.

00:04:17.790 --> 00:04:19.725
He came over
when he was, like, 11.

00:04:19.725 --> 00:04:22.861
But, yeah, his accent
gets stronger every year.

00:04:22.861 --> 00:04:24.863
And his --
This Greek culture.

00:04:24.863 --> 00:04:27.566
It's just like --
You know, he, like,

00:04:27.566 --> 00:04:28.968
just stinks of olive oil.

00:04:28.968 --> 00:04:30.837
Like, he has olives
coming out of his ears.

00:04:30.837 --> 00:04:32.638
And like --

00:04:32.638 --> 00:04:34.807
-Now, I -- You, obviously,

00:04:34.807 --> 00:04:37.510
find a lot of humor
in your parents,

00:04:37.510 --> 00:04:40.546
but I've heard your family --
very serious people,

00:04:40.546 --> 00:04:43.449
other than you and your brother.
-Oh, yeah, no.

00:04:43.449 --> 00:04:45.818
They don't like that --
Humor is not a big deal.

00:04:45.818 --> 00:04:48.220
It's like,
"I went to the shop today,

00:04:48.220 --> 00:04:53.192
and the milk was more expensive
than I had hoped."

00:04:53.192 --> 00:04:56.262
And, you know, everything
is very dramatic.

00:04:56.262 --> 00:04:57.996
'Cause they're Greek.
It's the ancient Greeks.

00:04:57.996 --> 00:04:59.732
You know, they're just, like --
Everything is

00:04:59.732 --> 00:05:01.501
so unbelievably serious.

00:05:01.501 --> 00:05:04.503
Like, my -- my...
my aunty --

00:05:04.503 --> 00:05:07.139
she has had problems
with her foot

00:05:07.139 --> 00:05:09.041
for as long as I can remember,
and it's just like --

00:05:09.041 --> 00:05:11.277
She's just got, like,
a bunion or something.

00:05:11.277 --> 00:05:12.644
But it's like,

00:05:12.644 --> 00:05:16.782
"My foot is very, very,
very gentle..."

00:05:16.782 --> 00:05:19.184
And, you know, like --
like, hold her foot in her hand.

00:05:19.184 --> 00:05:22.054
It's like, "Touch it
with your young skin. Please."

00:05:22.054 --> 00:05:24.523
[ Laughter ]

00:05:24.523 --> 00:05:27.593
-Do they -- Are they --
Are they happy that you are --

00:05:27.593 --> 00:05:29.629
you have a thriving career
in comedy?

00:05:29.629 --> 00:05:32.197
Does that mean anything to them?

00:05:32.197 --> 00:05:35.067
-I mean --
I mean, they know that, like,

00:05:35.067 --> 00:05:37.136
me and my brother
are on TV and we do stuff,

00:05:37.136 --> 00:05:40.439
but I think for them --

00:05:40.439 --> 00:05:42.975
The thing that would
really light them up --

00:05:42.975 --> 00:05:45.311
You know, they're just like,
"So is it 'The X Factor'?"

00:05:45.311 --> 00:05:47.814
And you're like,
"No, it's not 'X Factor.'

00:05:47.814 --> 00:05:51.817
It's a -- It's a mockumentary
written by Jemaine Clement,

00:05:51.817 --> 00:05:54.120
Taika Waititi,
Paul Simms, Stefani Robinson."

00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:55.521
You know, like your -- your --

00:05:55.521 --> 00:05:57.923
And they're like, "Yeah,
but what about Simon Cowell?"

00:05:57.923 --> 00:06:00.659
And, you know, they --

00:06:00.659 --> 00:06:03.496
I think to them
TV is like "X Factor,"

00:06:03.496 --> 00:06:05.398
"Britain's Got Talent,"
"America's Got Talent."

00:06:05.398 --> 00:06:07.066
You know, that -- or like --

00:06:07.066 --> 00:06:08.434
Or like the equivalent of, like,

00:06:08.434 --> 00:06:09.802
"The Bold and the Beautiful"
in the U.K.

00:06:09.802 --> 00:06:12.538
would be like "Coronation
Street" or something.

00:06:12.538 --> 00:06:15.641
And I think if I was on that,
they'd be like,

00:06:15.641 --> 00:06:17.910
"Bloody hell! Wow!"

00:06:17.910 --> 00:06:19.745
But, for this,
they're just like,

00:06:19.745 --> 00:06:22.648
"It's something
about, uh, dinosaurs."

00:06:22.648 --> 00:06:24.117
Something. An old --

00:06:24.117 --> 00:06:26.452
She's playing some --
an old thing."

00:06:26.452 --> 00:06:29.621
-Your -- your brother
Jamie's been on the show.

00:06:29.621 --> 00:06:31.323
He's incredibly talented.

00:06:31.323 --> 00:06:35.394
You are both in a wonderful show
called "Stath Lets...Flats."

00:06:35.394 --> 00:06:38.730
Sorry. It's a tongue --
It's a tongue twister.

00:06:38.730 --> 00:06:40.232
-Yeah.
-Do you enjoy --

00:06:40.232 --> 00:06:42.602
I've been lucky enough over the
years to work with my brother.

00:06:42.602 --> 00:06:44.336
Do you guys
enjoy working together?

00:06:44.336 --> 00:06:46.806
Is it more or less stressful?

00:06:46.806 --> 00:06:48.941
-Oh, it's -- I mean, it's --

00:06:48.941 --> 00:06:50.676
I mean, you must know.
It's just so lovely.

00:06:50.676 --> 00:06:54.113
'Cause, like, I think in this
industry you can sometimes --

00:06:54.113 --> 00:06:55.981
I don't know if anyone's
heard of this before --

00:06:55.981 --> 00:06:59.552
but people can be a little bit
fake in this industry.

00:06:59.552 --> 00:07:03.623
And it's so lovely
to be, like, on set

00:07:03.623 --> 00:07:05.624
with someone that you're like,
"God, I actually really love you

00:07:05.624 --> 00:07:08.127
'cause you're my family."

00:07:08.127 --> 00:07:10.763
That's so lovely.
And then, you know, we --

00:07:10.763 --> 00:07:13.098
It's really great to network
with him at Christmas,

00:07:13.098 --> 00:07:14.767
you know,
in front of my parents.

00:07:14.767 --> 00:07:17.703
-That's probably why -- I heard
that some people resent the fact

00:07:17.703 --> 00:07:19.972
that you maybe networked
your way into the part.

00:07:19.972 --> 00:07:22.408
-[ Laughs ] Yeah.

00:07:22.408 --> 00:07:24.110
Yeah, that -- that --
I do get that.

00:07:24.110 --> 00:07:26.912
But, yeah, so, no.
It's so heavenly.

00:07:26.912 --> 00:07:28.848
And I just -- I just --

00:07:28.848 --> 00:07:30.315
I really do.
I love him too much.

00:07:30.315 --> 00:07:31.950
Like, that's the only
stressful thing,

00:07:31.950 --> 00:07:33.586
is that sometimes
I want to cradle him

00:07:33.586 --> 00:07:36.955
like the tiny baby boy
he is in my eyes.

00:07:36.955 --> 00:07:39.025
But, you know, he's doing a job,
and it's really unhelpful

00:07:39.025 --> 00:07:40.926
when I'm trying to cradle him
and he's trying to film a scene.

00:07:40.926 --> 00:07:42.194
-So, it -- Because I --

00:07:42.194 --> 00:07:44.029
I have read him say
over the years --

00:07:44.029 --> 00:07:46.599
because you're
the older sibling --

00:07:46.599 --> 00:07:49.701
that, one, you guys
made each other laugh a lot,

00:07:49.701 --> 00:07:51.971
but, two, that he was just sort
of desperate to be around you,

00:07:51.971 --> 00:07:54.407
which I think is true
of most younger siblings.

00:07:54.407 --> 00:07:56.175
-Yeah, most people
are desperate

00:07:56.175 --> 00:07:57.843
to be around me, to be honest.

00:07:57.843 --> 00:07:59.278
-Yeah. I'm getting that.

00:07:59.278 --> 00:08:01.747
I will say. On this first
meeting, I'm getting that.

00:08:01.747 --> 00:08:03.249
-No. Yeah.

00:08:03.249 --> 00:08:06.852
It was -- Everyone always says
that they think he's older,

00:08:06.852 --> 00:08:11.690
so let's not forget that I am,
unfortunately, older.

00:08:11.690 --> 00:08:14.160
Yeah, he -- it --
it was -- we did just --

00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:16.295
I guess,
he is the only other person

00:08:16.295 --> 00:08:17.997
to live the way we've lived.

00:08:17.997 --> 00:08:19.765
I don't -- You know, like,
with the sort of

00:08:19.765 --> 00:08:22.235
weird stuff your parents do
in your upbringing.

00:08:22.235 --> 00:08:25.604
And so it's just, like, this
frame of reference is, like --

00:08:25.604 --> 00:08:27.706
I can just be like,
"Do that thing

00:08:27.706 --> 00:08:31.310
that Uncle Tony did
in, like, 1996."

00:08:31.310 --> 00:08:33.746
And he's like, "Yeah, okay.
Yeah, I'll do that."

00:08:33.746 --> 00:08:36.282
And then...
[ Laughs ]

00:08:36.282 --> 00:08:38.084
-Sophie,
your character on the show,

00:08:38.084 --> 00:08:43.155
is both very sweet and perhaps
not the brightest character.

00:08:43.155 --> 00:08:47.326
Do you enjoy that combination
of, like, a very good soul

00:08:47.326 --> 00:08:51.897
who maybe lacks intelligence?

00:08:51.897 --> 00:08:55.067
-Oh, yeah. I think playing --

00:08:55.067 --> 00:08:59.605
playing people
who are maybe stupid

00:08:59.605 --> 00:09:03.009
or a little bit, like, you know,
people who are, like, not --

00:09:03.009 --> 00:09:05.177
who, like, don't have
self-awareness

00:09:05.177 --> 00:09:08.380
and who aren't ashamed of it,
who just own it.

00:09:08.380 --> 00:09:12.785
You know, Sophie's just like,
"Look here. Look.

00:09:12.785 --> 00:09:14.686
I want to dance now,
so I'm gonna have a dance."

00:09:14.686 --> 00:09:16.955
And, like,
I just think that is --

00:09:16.955 --> 00:09:18.624
That is the funnest stuff
to play

00:09:18.624 --> 00:09:20.526
because being someone who's,
like, got all their [bleep]

00:09:20.526 --> 00:09:22.728
together, you know,
is a know-it-all,

00:09:22.728 --> 00:09:25.898
has all the answers,
they're boring!

00:09:25.898 --> 00:09:28.968
So -- And you're just like,
"Oh, great, this guy."

00:09:28.968 --> 00:09:32.571
So I think to play someone
who's just like,

00:09:32.571 --> 00:09:34.506
"I'm really stupid,
and I don't care,"

00:09:34.506 --> 00:09:35.975
is, like, heaven.

00:09:35.975 --> 00:09:38.477
'Cause it just --
I can just empty my brain

00:09:38.477 --> 00:09:41.814
and just be like,
"What would a slug do?"

00:09:41.814 --> 00:09:44.049
And then that's how I do
that character.

00:09:44.049 --> 00:09:46.151
-Well, it's heaven to watch,
as well.

00:09:46.151 --> 00:09:48.054
Thank you so much
for being here.

00:09:48.054 --> 00:09:49.554
Both shows are so fantastic,

00:09:49.554 --> 00:09:52.857
and I'm really happy
we got a chance to meet.

00:09:52.857 --> 00:09:55.060
-Aww. Thanks so much.

00:09:55.060 --> 00:09:57.996
-Hopefully next time in person.
"What We Do in the Shadows"

00:09:57.996 --> 00:10:00.066
airs Thursdays
at 10:00 p.m. on FX.

